## 1. Introduction
Today’s society is facing growing concerns about the global energy and environmental challenges initiated by the consumption of limited fossil fuels.[1-3] Among the next-generation energy technologies, fuel cells and metal-air batteries show great potential to meet our growing energy demands.[1,4,5] However, their overall efficiency is severely limited by the sluggish oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), a key process in fuel cells and metal-air batteries.[6,7] In the ORR, molecular oxygen is electrochemically reduced by a 4-electron pathway to $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ (acidic media) or $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ (alkaline media), or by a 2-electron pathway to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (acidic media) or $\mathrm{HO}_{2}^{-}$ (alkaline media) as the intermediate species (Figure 1).[8-10] Usually, as a consequence of the higher reaction kinetics and efficiency, the 4-electron pathway is preferred. So far, platinum-based materials are regarded as ideal ORR electrocatalysts with a 4-electron pathway in practical applications. However, the scarcity, high cost, and inferior durability of Pt restrict the widespread application in these renewable energy technologies.[10,11] Considering these limitations, developing earth-abundant alternatives with high activity and durability is a crucial yet challenging task.
As one new class of porous materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) interconnected by the coordination bonds of metal ions/clusters and organic ligands possess high crystallinity and long-range order.[12] In view of their intriguing features of high specific surface area, adjustable pore sizes and topologies, well-defined metal nodes, tunable chemical components, and surface functionality, MOFbased materials have been proved highly competitive to other porous materials as electrocatalysts towards ORR.[13-15] According to the chemical nature, MOF-based ORR electrocatalysts are divided into three categories: pristine MOFs, MOF composites, and their derivatives (Figure 2). The combination of porous channels in MOFs with accessible sites possessing redox ability may enable a higher active site utilization than typical heterogeneous catalysts. However, insufficient electrical conductivity and low chemical stability seriously limit their electrocatalytic applications.[16-18] Integrating MOFs with advanced functional materials, including nanocarbons and inorganic nanoparticles (NPs), is an efficient method to obtain remarkable performance in electrocatalysis. Converting MOFs or MOF composites to inorganic composites by direct carbonization or indirect post-treatments is another simple and efficient approach. Owing to the structural and compositional advantages of MOFs, their derivatives usually display a high surface area, good electrical conductivity, and robust chemical stability.[19-21] Under these guidelines, various MOF-based materials have been developed and well summarized in the past five years. However, these reviews mainly focus on MOFs and/or their derivatives for energy storage/conversion applications.[16,19,20] Few reviews paid focused attention to the relationship of MOFs, MOF composites, and their derivatives towards ORR. Therefore, a timely and comprehensive review on the synthesis strategies, structure, composition, morphology, electrocatalytic performance, reaction mechanism, and practical application is noteworthy, which can provide constructive guidance and strong inspiration for the development of MOF-based ORR electrocatalysts and their applications in energyrelated devices.
In this minireview, we outline the recent advances of MOFs, MOF composites and their derivatives as electrocatalysts towards ORR. Recent breakthroughs in engineering MOF-based ORR electrocatalysts are highlighted with an emphasis on synthesis strategy, component manipulation, morphology/structural control, and electrocatalytic performance evaluation. These MOF-based functional materials offer unprecedented opportunities in replacing precious-metal-based electrocatalysts towards ORR. Finally, this minireview is concluded with some major current challenges along with some solutions and potential development trends surrounding MOFs as advanced electrocatalysts towards ORR. We believe that this minireview will inspire extensive interest to further accelerate and explore the innovations of MOF-based materials in ORR-related energy technologies.
