## 2. Classification of MOF-based ORR catalysts
MOFs being a relatively young but rapidly-growing family of porous materials have sparked enormous research interest in chemistry and materials science due to their attractive properties.[22] So far, more than 20,000 MOFs have been reported, and the number of MOFs continues to grow exponentially assisted by the rapid development in synthetic methodologies, characterization techniques, and computational chemistry.[12] The typical synthesis of MOFs involves solvothermal methods, where separate metal salt and ligand solutions are mixed together or a solvent is added to the mixture of solid salt and ligand in a reaction vial. Apart from solvothermal methods, other methods such as microwave-assisted synthesis, mechanochemical synthesis, electrochemical synthesis, and sonochemical synthesis have also been developed for synthesis of MOFs.[23] Recently, MOF-based materials as electrocatalysts towards ORR have exhibited distinctive performance. Herein, using a classification of MOFs, MOF composites and their derivatives, we summarize their recent progresses in ORR and highlight some guiding works with an up-to-date appraisal.
## 2.1. Pristine MOFs
As a subclass of the broader coordination polymer family, MOFs have three important characteristics of crystallinity, porosity, and strong metal-ligand interactions, making them as a bridge between homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts.[24,25] The periodic structure allows MOFs to uniformly disperse the active sites throughout the porous framework, the well-defined characteristic of homogeneous catalysts, while the solid nature in nano/micro-scale endows MOFs as the promising heterogeneous catalysts.[16,24-27] Therefore, the rational design of MOFs could meet the benefits of both heterogeneous and homogeneous catalysts in one system.
Mao et al. pioneered in using pristine MOFs as electrocatalysts towards ORR with a Cu-based MOF (i.e., Cu-bipy-BTC, bipy $\mathbf{\Sigma}=2,2^{\circ}$ -bipyridine, $\mathbf{B}\mathrm{TC}=1,3,5$ -tricarboxylate).[28] As shown in Figure 3A, the specific coordination mode of $\mathrm{Cu^{II}}$ centers renders the structural stability in neutral solution. The cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves demonstrated the electrocatalytic activity towards ORR (Figure 3B). Subsequently, Kamiya et al. reported a hybrid ORR catalyst of copper-modified covalent triazine frameworks (CTFs) and carbon NPs.[29] Although both works suggest the possibility of MOFs as electrocatalysts towards ORR, the development of pristine MOFs in ORR is still dawdling. Recent investigations indicate that homogenous molecular catalysts with ORR activity can be grafted into MOFs as active linkers to catalyze ORR.[30] For instance, Lions et al. investigated the electrocatalytic ORR performance of Co-Al-PMOF with a molecular formula of $\mathrm{\small{[Al_{2}(O H)_{2}(C o(t c p p))]}}$ (tccp $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ tetrakis(4- carboxyphenyl)porphyrin).[31] As the porphyrin metalated analog of $[\mathrm{Al_{2}(O H)_{2}(H_{2}t c p p)}]$ $\mathrm{\ddot{H}}_{2}$ -Al-PMOF) (Figure 3C), it exhibits remarkable stability in an acidic medium.[32] Electrochemical characterization showed that the Co-Al-PMOF displayed an onset potential (Eonset) of $0.75\mathrm{V}$ versus reversible hydrogen electrode (vs RHE) and a diffusion limiting current density $(j_{L})$ of $-0.6\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in an $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ (Figure 3D). In the same year, Morris et al. reported a highly robust MOF named PCN-223-Fe by combining $\mathrm{{Zr}_{6}}$ oxo clusters and Fe(III) porphyrin linkers, opening a new route to optimize MOFs as ORR electrocatalysts by selecting proton sources.[33] These results exemplify that it is feasible to use ORR active complexes as the building blocks for preparing ORR-active MOFs. Unfortunately, most of these metalloporphyrin-based MOFs show poor performances due to the difficulties in maintaining and/or improving the ORR kinetics of the molecular complex in MOFs.
High conductivity is another desired important requirement for MOFs as electrocatalysts. However, MOFs are generally insulating due to the poor conduction of the redox-active sites surrounded by the typical organic linkers. Besides, the poor electronic interaction between metal nodes and organic linkers is another factor affecting the conductivity of MOFs.[17] In the recent five years, a few approaches have been realized for the construction of conductive MOFs.[17,34] In 2016, Miner et al. reported a twodimensional (2D) MOF named $\mathrm{{Ni}}_{3}(\mathrm{{hitp})}_{2}$ (hitp = 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminotriphenylene) with a high electrical conductivity of $40\thinspace\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ .[35] As shown in Figure 3E, $\mathrm{{Ni}}_{3}(\mathrm{{hitp})_{2}}$ has a honeycomb-like layered structure and exhibits good electrocatalytic activity in an extensive range of $\mathrm{pH}$ value (4-13) (Figure 3F). Intensive research indicated that $\mathrm{{Ni}}_{3}(\mathrm{{hitp})}_{2}$ catalyzed oxygen reduction through a two-electron pathway by the ligands as active sites rather than metal centers (Figure 3G).[36] This mechanism shows the importance of electron delocalization for accessing key intermediates and should be applicable for designing other conductive MOFs. Inspired by this work, Liu et al. designed two metal-catecholates with well-defined Ni-O6 or ${\mathrm{Co}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{O}}_{6}$ coordination sites.[37] Unlike metal-nitrogen coordination sites in the above MOFs as active sites for ORR, the specific metal-O6 sites in metal-catecholates can also function as active sites to reduce oxygen via a four-electron pathway.
Despite the successful demonstration of pristine MOFs as the emerging electrocatalysts towards ORR, their instability in electrochemical environment and poor electrical conductivity severely restrict their swift development in electrocatalysis. So far, one main strategy for improving the chemical stability of MOFs is to increase the bonding strength of metal-ligand coordination. In this case, some MOFs composed of high-oxidation metal ions and carboxylate ligands, or soft metal ions and basic azolate ligands have been developed.[38,39] For the issue of poor electrical conductivity, some strategies have been developed, such as donor-acceptor interactions, redox-matching, and mixed valency.[17,40] Recent investigations reported that in-situ growth of ultrathin MOF nanosheets on conducting substrates could also partly overcome the poor conductivity problem of pristine MOFs.[41-43]
## 2.2. MOF composites
Integrating MOFs with various functional materials is an effective and viable strategy to improve ORR performance along with new functionality.[22,44-47] To date, numerous MOF composites have been successfully obtained by assembling MOFs with functional materials, which can be divided into two
