nano-CuS) composites (Figure 4F).[52] Interestingly, the increasement of electrically conducting nanoCuS NPs in the composite resulted in a decrease in porosity but an increase in electrical conductivity. The optimized composite with $20\mathrm{\wt}\%$ CuS NPs exhibited an Eonset of 0.91 V vs RHE in an alkaline solution (Figure 4G). This work revealed the importance of both porosity and electrical conductivity to the electrocatalytic performance. The uniform and adjustable pores in MOFs can not only act as reaction chambers to confine noble metallic NPs but also more importantly limit the immersion of other bigger molecules such as methanol, thus providing a good model for designing efficient and selective electrocatalysts towards ORR.[56]
Benefiting from the functional materials and sophisticated architectures, these MOF composites possess not only enhanced electrical conductivity but also robust chemical stability. More notably, the synergistic effect from the interaction of component and structure enables these composites to possess new physical and chemical properties that are not present in the individual components, resulting in great activity and stability. Moreover, the abundance of MOFs and functional materials together with the development of new synthesis methods will create more advanced MOF composites with high electrocatalytic activity and stability.
## 2.3. MOF derivatives
In comparison to the attempts to adapt MOFs and MOF composites for ORR, much more efforts have been devoted to converting them into inorganic functional materials with controllable size, morphology, and composition.[19,57] On the one hand, the diversity of metal ions and ligands in MOFs renders them ideal precursors for preparing functional inorganic materials with various components. On the other hand, the flexible topology and size of MOFs also make them interesting precursors/templates to obtain the derived materials with desired morphology, structure and size. As described below, these MOF-derived materials often possess some unique advantages over the traditional porous materials. (1) The size, morphology, structure and other features inherited from the MOFs can be effectively preserved, enriching the database of structure and composition for porous materials.[21,58] (2) The carbon matrix pyrolyzed from the organic ligands possesses high conductivity and specific area, promoting the charge transfer and mass transport.[59] (3) Various heteroatoms can be directly doped into the carbon matrix through the organic ligands, which can not only induce the charge redistribution in the carbon matrix, but also interact with other functional components, producing more active sites.[60] (4) The metal atoms can evolve into ultrafine metal and metal compound NPs confined in the carbon network, preventing them from further agglomeration and sintering.[15,61] On the basis of chemical composition, we divided these derivatives into the following three categories to summarize their recent progresses as electrocatalysts towards ORR. Herein, we highlight recent works that have guiding significance in the synthesis strategy, morphology/structure control, component manipulation, and practical application in fuel cells and metalair batteries.
## 2.3.1. Metallic compounds
Due to the natural advantages of abundant metal species, MOFs can be easily transformed into metallic composites, which are promising alternatives to Pt-based catalysts in ORR. However, their intrinsically poor electrical conductivity and low specific area lead to insufficient activity.[62,63] Extensive efforts have been devoted to improving their catalytic activities by nanostructure engineering or compounding with highly conductive materials.[62,64] With various topographical dimensions and tunable sizes, MOFs are often used as desirable templates and/or precursors to obtain wanted nanostructures. When the organic ligands in MOFs are consumed by calcination in air or chemical etching, metallic compounds without carbon will be obtained as the first category of MOF derivatives.[65-68] More importantly, the consumption of organic ligands in MOFs will generally create high specific area and porosity at the same time, which are critical to expose more active sites.
