Metal oxides are the most widely studied class of compounds for ORR in view of their easy preparation and considerable stability in an oxidative or alkaline environment. Recently, Prussian blue analogues (PBAs) have been recognized as promising precursors for the synthesis of porous metal oxides.[69,70] Nai et al. explored the mechanism of assembling anisotropic PBA NPs into open frame-like superstructures and prepared two typical nanocuboids-assembled frame-like superstructure (NAFS) of Co-Fe mixed oxides by thermal treatment of  Co-Fe PBA NAFSs in air.[71] The formation process and mechanism of the KCoFe-1 NAFSs (a PAB with $\mathrm{K}/\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Fe}=0.67;1{:}0.71)$ and KCoFe-2 (a PAB with $\mathrm{K}/\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Fe}=\ 0.07;1{:}0.67;$ ) NAFSs were well investigated by time-dependent experiments, which shows the structural evolution process from NPs to  NAFSs (Figure 5A,B). While annealing KCoFe-1 and KCoFe-2 NAFSs in the air, they obtained the Co-Fe oxides with the uniform 3D cubic frame-like structures (Figure 5C-H). Subsequently, they also developed a self-templated epitaxial growth strategy for the construction of single-crystalline hollow nanostructured PBAs.[72] Due to the tunable growth kinetics and topological complexity, this strategy enables a controllable synthesis of Co-Fe PBA cages, frames, and boxes with diverse geometries. By simple annealing these PBAs with tailorable topologies in air, they obtained the corresponding Co-Fe mixed oxides (Co-Fe-O) with nanostructures of frames, cages, and boxes (Figure 5I-N).
Although these porous oxides can be further evolved into other slightly more conductive compounds (e.g., carbides, nitrides, phosphides, and selenides),[67,73] their conductivity is still insufficient to meet the needs of electrocatalysis, especially compared to carbon-based materials. In fact, in order to obtain more efficient and stable electrocatalysts, the organic ligands in these MOFs are mostly retained in the form of inorganic carbon-based materials.
