## 2.3.2. Carbon materials
In view of the great diversity of MOFs, pyrolysis of MOFs has been demonstrated to be a promising method for preparing novel carbon-based materials with controllable size, shape, and composition by choosing MOF precursors and calcination conditions.[59] MOF-derived carbon-based ORR electrocatalysts can be divided further into two categories: pristine carbon materials with defects and heteroatom-doped carbon materials. Before recognizing the function of defects in the ORR, it is generally believed that the pristine carbons show little electrocatalytic activity towards ORR due to the $s p^{2}$ -bonded carbon atoms without polarization, which cannot accommodate the chemical adsorption of reactants and electronic attack of intermediates.[74,75] However, recent studies revealed that some carbons without heteroatom dopants still exhibit excellent ORR activity, in which the topological defects can break the $s p^{2}$ orbitals of pristine carbon and generate charged sites as potential active sites for ORR.[76,77] Even though significant advances have been achieved by introducing defects in pristine carbons to boost the ORR activity, their activity is still relatively low and some critical issues remain to be addressed: 1) the identification of the most active defects towards ORR; 2) the precise control of the local structure of defects.
In contrast, the doping of heteroatoms is an appealing strategy, which can not only create defects but also easily cause changes in the electronic structure of surrounding carbon materials, thus introducing more active sites.[60,78,79] Since the first report by Dai and coworkers that N-doped carbon nanotubes possess superior ORR activity and stability compared to platinum,[79] various heteroatom-doped carbonbased electrocatalysts were reported. These heteroatoms include non-metallic elements (e.g., nitrogen, boron, sulfur, phosphorus, iodine) and metallic elements. Among the metal-free doped carbons, N-doped porous carbon was the first reported MOF-derived non-metallic atom-doped ORR electrocatalyst.[80] Nanostructure engineering is an effective strategy to enhance the electrocatalytic performance by exposing more active sites and accelerating electron/mass transfer. In this context, Yu and coworkers reported N-doped carbon nanofibers by calcinating 1D ZIF-8 nanofibers, which are prepared by using Te nanowires as the template (Figure 6A).[81] These novel porous carbon nanofibers possess a complex network structure with hierarchical pores and high surface area (Figure 6B). Coincidentally, a bottomup method was proposed by Chai et al. for synthesizing hollow N-doped carbon spheres (HNCSs) by using Zn-BTC microspheres as precursors (Figure 6C).[82] The scanning electron microscope (SEM) and TEM images (Figure 6D) showed the unique hollow architecture with numerous mesopores, which facilitates the diffusion and access of electrolyte and oxygen molecules, also exposing more active sites for ORR. Although many studies have been conducted on the carbonization of MOFs, few of them have examined the influence of annealing atmosphere on the structure of derivatives. In 2015, Xia et al. prepared N-doped carbon nanotube frameworks (NCNTFs) by direct pyrolysis of ZIF-67 particles under an $\mathrm{Ar/H}_{2}$ atmosphere (Figure 6E).[83] Benefiting from the features of composition and structure, the optimized NCNTFs could catalyze the reduction of oxygen molecules by a nearly 4-electron pathway with higher electrocatalytic activity than that of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ in alkaline solution. The success of N doping has triggered the study of other dopants, such as B, P, S and I. Moreover, simultaneous doping of two or more elements with anti-electronegativity into the resultant carbon matrix can induce unique electron-donor properties, creating more defects and active sites for ORR.[78,84,85] Remarkably, a controllable multi-heteroatoms (B, N, P, S) doped porous carbon nanofibrous network was prepared by Huang et al. using a 3D network of ZIF-8/polymeric nanofiber as the template (Figure 6F).[86] By controlling the absorption amount of $\mathrm{\bfB}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{HPO}_{4}{}^{2-}$ , and $\mathrm{SO}_{4}{}^{2-}$ , single, dual, ternary, and quaternary heteroatoms doped carbons were obtained, suggesting high degree of doping capability.
Compared to the development of metal-free carbon materials in ORR, metal-doped carbon materials have received even more attention, in which the metal atoms can be well dispersed in the resultant porous
