## 2.3.3. Metallic compound/carbon hybrids
When the metal in the above-mentioned metal-doped carbons grows from the atomic scale to the molecular scale, the third category of MOF-derived materials is produced, that is, metal or metallic compound/carbon hybrids. Due to the reductive atmosphere created by the pyrolytic carbon from MOFs, abundant metal atoms can be reduced into metallic NPs via calcination in inert atmosphere under high temperature.[100-102] The derived carbon matrix (especially heteroatom-doped carbons) works as a support for metallic NPs and prevents their aggregation.[101] At the same time, the strong interaction between metallic NPs and the carbon support can tune the electronic structure and enhance the electrocatalytic activity.[103] In turn, metallic NPs can also increase the degree of graphitization of the carbon matrix, improve the protection of carbon on metallic NPs, and avoid their dissolution during electrocatalysis.[82] For example, Guan et al. developed a MOF composite-assisted strategy for the synthesis of single-holed cobalt/N-doped carbon (Co/NC) hollow particles by controlled pyrolysis of yolk-shelled polystyrene $@$ ZIF-67 (PS $@$ ZIF-67) composites (Figure 8A).[101] The samples retained their spherical morphology after carbonization with a single large hole on each shell, in which Co nanocrystals were uniformly distributed (Figure 8B,C).
Compared to the single metals, metal alloys exhibit superior electrocatalytic activity due to the intrinsic polarity between different metals which can promote catalytic reactions.[104,105] One typical example is the preparation of ultra-low-loading precious-metal-based alloys/carbon composites from MOFs precursors for proton-exchange membrane fuel cells (PEMFC). Recently, Liu and coworkers used ZIF-67 or ZIF-8 $@$ ZIF-67 as precursors to obtain uniformly dispersed PtCo alloy NPs over a substrate of dense Co-Nx-Cy sites and onion-like graphitic layers encapsulated Co nanocrystals (Figure 8D).[106] High
