angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) images show that the PtCo alloy NPs are surrounded by individual Co atoms and a trace amount of $\mathrm{Pt}$ atoms (Figure 8E,F). High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) reveals the PtCo core and Pt shell in alloy NPs (Figure 8G). The LSV curves show the increased ORR activity of PF-2 (PF: platinum-group metal (PGM)-free) $\prec$ Pt3Co/ZC (ZC: ZIF-8-derived carbon) $\leq$ commercial $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}<\mathrm{LP}@\mathrm{PF}-1$ (LP $@$ PF: PtCo alloy NPs with an ultralow concentration of Pt supported over PGM-free materials) < LP@PF-2, and the electron transfer number for both ORR of LP $@$ PF-1 and LP $@$ PF-2 is 3.99 (Figure 8H). When LP@PF1 and LP $@$ PF-2 were incorporated in the cathode of the membrane electrode assembly (MEA), both displayed higher catalytic activities than the comparative MEAs within the high-voltage regions in a H2- O2 cell (Figure 8I). Moreover, these two MEAs both showed higher mass activities (MAs) than those of comparative MEAs, and LP@PF-1 MEA possessed the highest durability after 30000 cycles (Figure 8J). Computational modeling studies revealed that the interaction between PtCo alloy NPs and Co- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf N_{x}}$ -Cy sites contributed to the satisfactory ORR activity and durability in the fuel cell.
Moreover, the carbonization temperature has a significant influence on the phase of MOF derivatives, particularly the Fe-based MOF derivatives.[107-109] During pyrolysis, iron ions are reduced to Fe NPs, as the temperature increases, carbon atoms from the surrounding shells diffuse into the Fe cores, forming $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}}C$ NPs. Noteworthily, Guan et al. prepared Fe3C NP-embedded N-doped carbon nanotube assemblies supported by a porous N-doped carbon matrix (Fe3C@N-CNT assemblies) using a dual-MOF confined-pyrolysis strategy (Figure 9A).[110] The introduction of a second MOF (MIL-88B nanorod) into a MOF host (ZIF-8 polyhedron) guaranteed a confined-pyrolysis process for the synthesis of uniform Fe3C $@$ N-CNT assemblies without aggregation of Fe3C NPs and metal Fe impurity (Figures 9B-D). The unique structure and composition lead to the remarkable ORR performance including electrocatalytic activity, durability, and methanol tolerance capability (Figure 9E-G). Furthermore, this dual-MOF precursor strategy can be used in the design and synthesis of many other hybrid functional materials, providing a new approach to the development of highly active MOF-derived porous materials for future energy storage and conversion technologies.
Besides the carbonization temperature, the detailed conversion conditions also have important influence on the composition of MOF derivatives, resulting in metal oxides, chalcogenides, phosphides, and nitrides. Recently, numerous MOF-derived metal oxide/carbon composites with desired nanostructure and composition have been prepared by the pyrolysis strategy under an inert atmosphere followed with an oxidation in air, such as $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -carbon porous nanowire arrays,[64]  Co@Co3O4@C core $@$ bishell NPs,[111] Co@Co3O4 encapsulated in CNT-grafted N-doped carbon polyhedra,[112] and CoCo3O4-based multi-level nanoarchitectures embedded in hollow N-doped carbon polyhedral.[113] Apart from cobalt oxides, manganese oxides have also drawn considerable interest for ORR because of their multiple 3d electron configurations and various morphologies and phases.[114-116] Most recently, Lu et al. engineered abundant MnO/Co heterointerfaces in porous graphitic carbon (MnO/Co/PGC) polyhedrons via one-step pyrolysis of a bimetal-organic framework (Figure 9H).[117] After calcination, the MnO/Co/PGC polyhedrons possess a rough surface with a distinct graininess (Figure 9I). The in-situ generated Co nanocrystals are uniformly distributed within porous graphitic carbon polyhedrons (Figure 9J), creating well-defined heterointerfaces. Owing to the desired composition and formation of the heterostructures, the resulting MnO/Co/PGC exhibits superior activity and stability towards ORR (Figure 9K). More importantly, the MnO/Co/PGC also shows high activity towards oxygen evolution reaction (OER), thus making it an efficient air cathode for the rechargeable Zn-air battery (ZAB). The homemade ZAB was able to deliver excellent performance including a peak power density of $172\mathrm{\mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and a specific capacity of 872 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 9L), as well as excellent cycling stability (350 cycles), outperforming the mixed commercial $\mathbf{Pt/C||RuO}_{2}$ catalysts (Figure 9M). A light-emitting diode screen
