Metal chalcogenides are usually formed by the interaction between the central metal (e.g., Co, Ni, Fe, Ir, Ru) and chalcogen atoms (e.g., S, Se, Te), resulting in a unique cluster-like crystal structure. These clusters can provide a good reservoir for electron transfer from catalysts to adsorb oxygen during ORR. Meanwhile, the chalcogenization can also lead to changes in the surface chemistry of the metals and prevent oxidation poisoning of the prepared electrocatalyst in an alkaline solution as well as dissolution in an acidic medium.[10,52] Metal sulfides are well studied due to the variety of sulfur sources and the simplicity of the vulcanization.[118,119] In 2015, Chen et al. reported the preparation of $\mathbf{CoxSy}@\mathbf{C}$ from ZIF-67.[120] Subsequently, Hu et al. adopted ZIF-67 as the templating precursor and thioacetamide as the sulfur source to construct novel Co NP-embedded carbon $@\mathrm{Co}95_{8}$ double-shelled nanocages (CoC@Co9S8 DSNCs) as electrocatalysts for ORR, where numerous particles were uniformly dispersed without aggregation (Figure 9N-P).[121] The unique architecture not only possesses uniformly distributed high-density active sites but also significantly shorten the diffusion pathway of the electrolyte and improve mass transport of the gas and electrolyte, thereby rendering them high catalytic activity and superior long-term stability for ORR. The introduction of a phosphorus or nitrogen source prior to MOF carbonization produces a highly conductive and stable phosphide or nitride, which also displays superior electrocatalytic activity and stability towards ORR. These phosphorus or nitrogen sources can be obtained by regulating organic ligands or introducing additional inorganic sources (e.g., Na2H2PO2, red phosphorus, phytic acid, $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , and urea).[58,66,122-125] Recently, Sun et al. prepared a hybrid nanostructure comprising Ni2P nanoparticles anchored on MOF-derived Co, N-codoped porous carbon polyhedrons, demonstrating excellent trifunctional electrocatalytic performances for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), OER and ORR.[122] Unlike this work using NaH2PO2 as the phosphorus source, Wang et al. selected a Cu-based MOF containing phosphorus atoms and nitrogen atoms in the ligands as the single precursor and template to prepare novel Cu3P NPs coated by a N, P-codoped carbon shell.[126] Recently, Guan et al. synthesized unique hybrid cobalt/cobalt nitride NPs well-integrated with N-doped carbon (NC-Co/CoNx) nanoarrays through nitridation of 2D layer-structured Co-ZIF-derived Co NP-decorated NC nanoarrays.[124] The well-integrated NC-Co/CoNx electrode combined the merits of every single component and delivered remarkable bifunctional activity towards both OER and ORR in alkaline media, thus rendering it a promising air cathode for ZABs.
For the synthesis of most MOF derivatives, two basic strategies are often employed to control the chemical composition: 1) tuning the composition of MOF precursors; 2) manipulating the conversion conditions. Certainly, these two strategies are adapted simultaneously in many cases to achieve superior electrocatalytic activity and stability. Thanks to the great diversity in MOFs and the conversion processes, these MOF derivatives usually possess multiple components and thus multi-functional electrocatalytic capability. Even so, the development of MOF derivatives is perhaps still in the infancy, and their electrocatalytic performance needs to be further improved. Therefore, the relation of material physicochemical properties with their electrocatalytic performance should be reflected while designing and constructing these MOF derivatives as electrocatalysts towards ORR. First, the component properties determine the intrinsic activity and conductivity. Second, modification of the materials’ morphology/structure can expose a greater number of active sites. Third, favorable structures can prevent catalysts from agglomeration and promote mass transfer, thus enabling high electrocatalytic activity and
