stability. With these points in mind, more efforts should be dedicated to exploring MOF derivatives for highly efficient ORR.
## 3. Conclusions and Perspectives
In summary, this minireview provides an overview of the recent developments of MOF-based materials as highly efficient electrocatalysts towards ORR, which is one important reaction in fuel cells and metalair batteries. An ideal electrocatalyst should possess several important features including high intrinsic catalytic activity (i.e., TOF), abundant exposed active sites (i.e., electrochemically active surface area (ECSA)), decent electron transfer and mass transport capabilities (i.e., Tafel slope), and durable stability.[127-129] As a class of newly emerging versatile materials, MOF-based materials provide great opportunities for obtaining various advanced electrocatalysts with desired morphologies, structures, compositions, and properties. Regarding their inherent characteristics, a variety of synthetic strategies have been developed to obtain highly efficient electrocatalysts for ORR, which can be generally summarized in the following aspects.
First, the ordered porous structure and solid character of pristine MOFs render them as a bridge between homogeneous and heterogeneous electrocatalysts. This provides an opportunity for studying the catalytic mechanism at the molecular level. In the context of electrocatalytic ORR, MOFs with a high degree of stability in aqueous solution and particularly with tolerance to varying pH conditions are desirable. For example, the bonding strength of metal-ligand coordination in some MOFs can be often enhanced with carboxylate-terminated linkers, tetra-carboxylate ligands and 4-connected Al(OH)(RCOO)2 chains and Fe(III) porphyrin linkers, which in terms leads to better chemical stability. High electrical conductivity is another important factor for improving the electrocatalytic activity and electron transfer number. Inspired by the high activity of the conductive 2D MOF, $\mathrm{{Ni}}_{3}(\mathrm{{hitp})}_{2}$ , more and more strategies, including redox-matching, donor-acceptor interactions and mixed valency as well as
This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
growing ultrathin 2D MOFs directly on conducting substrate, have been used to improve the electrical conductivity thus leading to higher catalytic efficiency.
Second, MOF composites integrated with many inorganic functional materials have been explored and efficiently utilized as electrocatalysts towards ORR. These functional materials usually possess higher electrical conductivity or electrocatalytic activity and stability, such as carbon materials (e.g., heteroatoms doped or undoped CNTs and graphene, as well as their composites), metallic NPs, metal oxides and sulfides. Each component in the MOF composites possesses a unique function. As a result, MOF composites can often overcome the drawbacks of the individual counterparts and generate synergistic effects for enhanced electrocatalytic performance. Moreover, the pores in the MOFs can act as gates to effectively regulate the contact of reactants and electrolytes with the catalyst, providing a good model for designing electrocatalysts with selective function.
Third, inheriting the remarkable features of MOFs to a great extent, the MOF derivatives have unique advantages in comparison to the traditional porous materials, such as simple and efficient preparation procedure, large specific surface area and porosity inherited from MOFs, facile doping of highly dispersed heteroatoms, and controllable size/morphology/component by pre-designing MOFs and MOF composites. Therefore, the derivation of MOFs and MOF composites opens a promising avenue to prepare diversified highly efficient electrocatalysts towards ORR. On the one hand, the organic ligands can be pyrolyzed to highly conductive porous carbon matrix with/without doping of heteroatoms, which can not only function as catalysts or catalyst supports for loading other active species but also guarantee the fast charge transfer/mass transport and the exposure of active sites. On the other hand, the metal elements at the nodes can be converted to various inorganic substances through direct calcination or posttreatment, such as single atoms, metal or metal alloys, metal carbides, oxides, sulfides, phosphides and nitrides. Moreover, compared to carbon materials, these single metal atoms, metal/alloy NPs and metallic
This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
compounds can be well dispersed in the resultant porous carbon, therefore improving the density of active sites. In addition, unique morphologies and structures are also generated in the micrometer/nanometer scale. These derivatives possess a large number of robust and highly active catalytic sites as a result of desired structure and component, where the adsorption mode of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules, cleavage style of O-O bonds and bonding strength of intermediates can be well optimized, leading to high electrocatalytic ORR activity and stability.
Overall, though incredible advances have been achieved, the development of MOF-based electrocatalysts towards ORR is perhaps still in the infancy and there are still many challenges for further investigations of more efficient MOF-based electrocatalysts towards ORR, some of which are listed below.
(1) Limited availability of MOFs for special uses, such as, highly conductive and stable MOFs as pristine electrocatalysts, and MOF precursors or templates for metal-free carbon-based derivatives.
(2) Large-scale synthesis of MOFs. The high price of ligands, harsh synthetic conditions for some MOFs, and limited number of synthesis methods severely hamper the development process from basic research to practical industrialization.
(3) Mechanistic studies are needed for the so-called synergistic effects in promoting catalytic activity enhancement in MOF composites and derived inorganic composites.
(4) Although there are some recent reports indicating that some pristine MOFs can be directly used for electrocatalysis, the surface of MOFs may undergo an irreversible phase change during the ORR process. Therefore, the real active sites should be identified by more advanced characterizations.
(5) Difficulty in preparing pure carbon materials derived from MOFs. Traces of metal often remain in MOF-derived metal-free carbon materials even after an acidic wash.
This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
(6) Precise control over the active sites, especially in MOF derivatives. These derivatives usually contain multiple phases due to the complex variability during carbonization and/or post-treatment processes.
(7) Accurate control and regulation of pore structure in MOF derivatives. The pyrolysis usually requires high temperature, which puts an obstacle in rationally controlled synthesis of derivatives with desired porous characteristics.
(8) Lack of understanding of the transformation process from MOFs to their derivatives. (9) The electrocatalytic activity and stability of MOF-based materials need to be further improved, especially in acidic media.
(10) Efficient and stable electrocatalysts with special requirements are highly desired for the practical applications of MOF-based materials in energy storage and conversion. For example, strong tolerance to contaminants (e.g., methanol, $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{x}}$ , halide ions, sulfur oxides, nitrogen oxides) for fuel cells, and robustness and bifunctionality for rechargeable metal-air batteries.
To overcome the above challenges, some solutions and potential development trends might be noted as follows.
(i) The synthesis of MOFs possessing strong metal-ligand coordination bond, redox activity, donoracceptor interactions, and mixed valency, is of great necessity. The coordination between the metal centers and organic ligands determines largely the stability, while other factors determine the conductivity.[30,38,39,130,131] Along this line, some MOFs such as porphyrin MOFs, phthalocyanine-based conjugated MOFs and UiO family ( $\mathbf{U}\mathbf{i}\mathbf{O}=\mathbf{\partial}$ Universitetet i Oslo) show great promise as pristine electrocatalysts towards ORR.
(ii) Covalent organic frameworks, with complete organic skeletons, provide great opportunities to facilely construct porous pure carbon materials with the precise control over heteroatom doping.[132-134]
This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
(iii) More facile approaches for the synthesis of MOF-based materials should be developed. For example, post-synthetic modifications of metal nodes and ligands of MOFs, such as multi-metal coordination and solvent-assisted ligand incorporation, should be used to enrich the types of MOFs.[131,135] Constructing more MOF composites (MOFs with metal clusters, polyoxometallates, NPs, semiconductor, and polymer) and postprocessing of derivatives are the other two popular ways.[19,47,57]
(iv) In-situ characterization techniques would be very helpful to understand the evolution of the electrocatalyst and the mechanism of the electrocatalytic reaction. Operando in-situ monitoring the growth of MOFs and the transformation process to derivatives is essential for developing new electrocatalysts.  Advanced testing techniques combining electrochemical measurements with in-situ spectroscopy (FT-IR, Raman, XPS, and EXAFS) or in-situ scanning probe microscopy can be helpful in the real-time analysis of the dynamic reaction mechanism of ORR.[136-138]
(v) With the advances of computer science and quantum chemistry, the combination of experimental and theoretical studies will give new insights into the synergetic effects and create many new opportunities for discovery and breakthroughs in advanced electrocatalysts.[87,139]
(vi) System-level studies would be appreciated for the design and synthesis of highly efficient electrocatalysts. Other than highly active sites, the density and utilization rate of active sites, charge transfer, interface properties between different components, electrocatalysts and electrolyte, etc., are all important factors for improving the overall electrocatalytic performance.[140,141]
(vii) Highly efficient and stable electrocatalysts in acidic media can be realized by incorporation of M-N-C moieties[90,95,99,142,143] or Pt-based alloys into MOFs, MOF composites, and MOF derivatives.[56,106,144]
(viii) The specific requirements of electrocatalysts for practical applications can sometimes be satisfied with the design of catalysts. For example, uniformly tunable pores in MOFs and mesoporous
This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
carbon wall in MOF derivatives can provide effective selectivity towards oxygen molecules but largely prevent crossover by contaminants.[56,106] For long-life rechargeable metal-air batteries, robust and bifunctional electrocatalysts should be aimed, which can be prepared by integrating the OER active sites and the ORR active sites in a single catalyst.[117,124,145] Equally important, considerable efforts should be devoted to the fabrication of devices with different electrolytes for practical applications in the future.[5,145,146]
In conclusion, MOFs will undoubtedly continue to spark more research on electrocatalysts for ORR and other reactions. From a practical point of view, MOF-based materials with earth-abundant elements should be employed to fully address the pressing challenges faced by our society in energy and environment. With persistent research efforts, we should have a good chance to witness a revolution of new technologies in renewable energy and environmental science in the near future.
